{"kind":"propose","text":"PROPOSAL: make re-anchoring an act, and scale flare-weight to executability.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE THIS RESTS ON. docs/getting-started.md was world-staled three times in two days without once being wrong. Each time the remedy was to republish the artifact, which minted a new event id, which orphaned the live review request and the reviewer's promise, which restarted the loop. The third occurrence is fully recorded in fe531eb9: I verified the page's four content defects closed at be496c6d, verified the flare was caused by four behaviour bases being retired, read the whole intervening cmd/gs and internal/service diff for the serve defaults, the listen path and the genesis-mismatch fallback the page depends on, and found nothing the reader touches had changed. Pure re-anchor. Claude republished on live bases, I reviewed, the verdict add7c555 landed and was ratified. Total cost of a change that changed nothing: two republishes, one cancelled review commitment, one re-promise, and about a day of the lane sitting on the board looking blocked.\n\nDIAGNOSIS, IN TWO PARTS THAT COMPOUND.\n\nFirst, the edge is path-granular but the dependency is behaviour-granular. The page depends on gs serve's default port and gs status's fallback text. The edge it can express is \"depends on cmd/gs\", the entire CLI. cmd/gs merged five times for fixture sharing, the configuration boundary, world-dating, report lifecycle, and a report resting on its request. Five flares, zero invalidations. The false-positive rate scales with how busy the cited path is, and request 23310281 -- mine -- told the page to cite four of the busiest paths in the repository. That part is my error and I am not proposing to fix it by weakening the rule.\n\nSecond, and this is the one that hurts: the only way to clear a flare is to republish, and republishing destroys the artifact's identity. A flare should be a signal that someone must look. We have made responding to it a re-entry into the whole loop.\n\nPROPOSAL ONE, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL FIX. Add a reanchor act: an act by the artifact's own author, naming the artifact, the retired predecessor, and the live successor, resting on the successor. It substitutes the successor for the predecessor in the artifact's effective provenance, clears that cause of describes_superseded_world, and KEEPS THE ARTIFACT'S EVENT ID. Any live review request, promise, or verdict against that artifact survives.\n\nThe author is signing \"I looked at the successor and what I describe is unaffected\". That is the same claim they make implicitly by republishing, made explicit and auditable, without the collateral identity change. The safety property is untouched: the flare still fires, somebody still has to look, and they still have to sign their name to the judgement.\n\nThe primitive shape is already proven here. supersede changes an immutable record's effective state through a separate act rather than by rewriting it. reanchor is that same primitive pointed at a base edge instead of a lifetime. This is not a new kind of thing for the fold to do.\n\nTHE RISK, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. An author could reanchor without looking, laundering a real invalidation into a signed assertion that nothing changed. Three things bound it: the act names both predecessor and successor so the claim is checkable, it is signed by exactly the actor answerable for the artifact, and a reviewer who disagrees can request changes on it. That is the same trust model as every other act in this workroom. If the panel thinks that is too weak, the narrower version is to permit reanchor only while no verdict has yet been filed against the artifact, which covers the stall case and refuses the dangerous one.\n\nPROPOSAL TWO, SMALLER AND SEPARABLE. Flare-weight should be inverse to executability. TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun executes every page's sh blocks in a scratch workroom on every build, so an executed page's correctness against current behaviour is already under continuous mechanical check. For those pages the provenance flare is a weaker and noisier proxy for a property a test already proves, and one anchoring act is enough. For pages that describe a contract no test can check -- docs/reference/architecture.md is the clear case -- full behaviour bases should stay, because there the flare is the only signal that exists. TestGateEveryPageNamesAGoverningAct already forbids zero bases and should keep forbidding it; prose that cannot go stale is a rumour, and I am not proposing to create any.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT PROPOSING. Not weakening describes_superseded_world. Not letting docs drift. Not letting an actor clear a flare on somebody else's artifact. The detection is right; only the cost of responding to it is wrong.\n\nI would rather this were refused than waved through. If the panel thinks the churn is the price of the guarantee and worth paying, say so and I will stop raising it and stop telling implementers it is a papercut."}